from pathlib import Path
import sys
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]/'authoring'))
from engine import Author
A=Author('27');add=A.add
add('6.1.1-01','An atomic nucleus contains protons and, for most nuclides, neutrons.','Protons have positive charge and neutrons have no net electric charge.','Electrons occupy the surrounding atomic region rather than being ordinary constituent nucleons. The common hydrogen-1 nucleus is a proton without a neutron, so avoid saying every nucleus must contain both. Nearly all atomic mass is concentrated in the nucleus, while the atom’s size is much larger than the nucleus.','Define a nucleon.','A nucleon is a proton or neutron in a nucleus.',heading='The atomic nucleus')
add('6.1.1-02','Proton number Z counts the protons in a nucleus.','It identifies the element.','Changing neutron number can produce another isotope of the same element, but changing proton number changes element identity. In a neutral atom, electron number equals Z; an ion can have a different electron number without changing its nucleus or element. Do not define Z by electrons without the neutrality condition.','Define proton number.','It is the number of protons in a nucleus.')
add('6.1.1-03','Nucleon number A counts protons plus neutrons in a nucleus.','Neutron number is A − Z.','A nucleus with 15 protons and 16 neutrons has A = 31. The mass number is an integer count, not a measured atomic mass in kilograms. Electrons are not included in A. A minus Z subtracts the proton count from the total, leaving the neutron count.','Define nucleon number.','It is the total number of protons and neutrons in a nucleus.',heading='Nucleon number A')
add('6.1.1-04','Nuclide notation places A at the upper left and Z at the lower left of the element symbol.','For phosphorus-31 the notation is ³¹₁₅P, with 15 protons and 16 neutrons.','Read the two positions independently: the upper number is the total nucleon count, while the lower is proton number. In plain text, phosphorus-31 with A = 31 and Z = 15 is an unambiguous accessible equivalent. Isotope notation does not itself specify how many electrons an ion has unless charge is also given.','Draw the nuclide notation for phosphorus with 15 protons and 16 neutrons.','Place A = 31 at upper left and Z = 15 at lower left of P.~The notation is ³¹₁₅P.')
add('6.1.1-05','Nuclear equations conserve total nucleon number and total electric charge.','Alpha emission reduces daughter A by four and Z by two; beta-minus emission leaves A unchanged and increases daughter Z by one.','An alpha particle is ⁴₂He. A beta-minus electron is represented ⁰₋₁e in nuclear-number accounting. For example, ²¹⁰₈₄Po → ²⁰⁶₈₂Pb + ⁴₂He, while ¹⁴₆C → ¹⁴₇N + ⁰₋₁e gives the school beta-number balance. A beta-minus process also emits an antineutrino, omitted from that simplified number-accounting display because it changes neither A nor charge sum.','Explain how nuclear-number conservation distinguishes alpha from beta-minus decay.','Link alpha emission to removal of A = 4 and Z = 2.~For beta-minus, the emitted electron has A = 0 and charge number −1, so daughter A stays fixed and Z increases by one.')
add('6.1.1-05-M01','Alpha emission removes two protons and two neutrons from the parent nucleus.','The daughter has Aparent − 4 and Zparent − 2.','Using the original practice choice ²²⁶₈₈Ra, the daughter is ²²²₈₆Rn. Check both balances: 226 equals 222 + 4 and 88 equals 86 + 2. The element symbol follows the new proton number; it is not unchanged merely because the same atom started the process.','Calculate the daughter’s A and Z in alpha decay of a nucleus with A = 226 and Z = 88.','A = 226 − 4 = 222.~Z = 88 − 2 = 86, giving ²²²₈₆Rn with an emitted ⁴₂He particle.')
add('6.1.1-05-M02','Beta-minus decay converts a neutron into a proton with an emitted electron and antineutrino.','The nucleus’s nucleon count is unchanged while its proton number rises by one.','A phosphorus-32 nucleus can become sulfur-32: ³²₁₅P → ³²₁₆S + ⁰₋₁e in the simplified school number display. Including the antineutrino completes the particle account without changing the displayed A and charge balances. The emitted electron is produced in the decay, not an ordinary atomic-shell electron falling out.','Calculate the daughter’s A and Z after beta-minus decay of a nucleus with A = 32 and Z = 15.','A remains 32 because the total nucleon count is unchanged.~Z becomes 16, so the daughter is ³²₁₆S.')
add('6.1.2-01','Isotopes are atoms of the same element with the same proton number but different nucleon numbers.','Their neutron numbers differ.','Carbon-12 and carbon-14 both have six protons, but have six and eight neutrons respectively. Neutral atoms of these isotopes have the same electron count, which supports similar chemical behaviour. Their nuclear stability can differ, so isotope does not mean automatically radioactive: some isotopes are stable.','Define isotopes.','They are atoms with the same proton number but different nucleon numbers.')
add('6.1.2-02','Radioisotope applications use detectable emission, penetration, ionisation or energy release.','The radiation type and half-life must suit the intended task.','A tracer should remain detectable for its measurement but need not persist far longer than required. A thickness monitor needs partial transmission that changes measurably with thickness. Sterilisation uses damage to microorganisms, while nuclear power commonly uses controlled fission of suitable fuel rather than merely detecting emissions. Name the actual mechanism for each application.','Explain why leak tracing and sterilisation require different uses of radioactive emission.','Link tracing to detecting the location of a moving labelled substance.~Sterilisation instead uses absorbed radiation to damage microorganisms.')
add('6.1.2-02-M01','Carbon-14 dating uses the decrease of radioactive carbon after an organism stops exchanging carbon with its surroundings.','The remaining activity or isotope ratio can be compared with an appropriate initial model.','Living material exchanges carbon; after death that exchange largely stops and the carbon-14 decays. More elapsed half-lives leave a smaller remaining fraction. Calibration and contamination matter in real dating, so the school exponential model is an approximation rather than an exact age for any arbitrary object. It does not date an unconnected metal sample merely because all objects contain atoms.','Explain the basis of carbon-14 dating of once-living material.','After carbon exchange stops, carbon-14 decays.~The remaining fraction compared with a calibrated starting model indicates elapsed time.')
add('6.1.2-02-M02','A radioactive tracer labels a substance so its location or movement can be detected.','Suitable penetrating emission can be measured by a detector outside the studied region.','In nuclear-medicine or biochemical examples, a selected labelled substance follows a process and its radiation provides information. Choice depends on chemical behaviour, emission and half-life. This describes the physical principle rather than selecting a patient’s tracer or dose. The tracer is the material introduced; the detector does not become the tracer.','Explain how a radioactive tracer reveals movement of a substance.','The isotope travels with the labelled substance.~Its emitted radiation is detected at locations or times that reveal the pathway.')
add('6.1.2-02-M03','Radiotherapy uses controlled radiation to damage targeted cells.','Absorption and ionisation can affect both intended targets and healthy tissue.','Specialist planning aims to concentrate the intended effect while limiting unwanted exposure. The physics link is radiation interaction with cells, not a rule that radiation recognises disease automatically. This syllabus example supplies no individual treatment or dose advice.','Explain why radiation used to damage cancerous cells also needs careful targeting.','The absorbed radiation can damage cells through ionisation.~Healthy cells can also be affected, so the intended exposure must be controlled.')
add('6.1.2-02-M04','A detectable radioactive tracer in a fluid can help locate a pipe leak.','A local change or peak in detector response can indicate where labelled fluid leaves its intended path.','Measure a suitable background and compare positions along the route. The isotope must follow the fluid and its emission must reach the detector under the stated conditions. Real use needs appropriate containment and authorised handling; the learning task interprets a supplied detector record rather than introducing material into public pipes.','Explain how a radioactive tracer can help locate a leak.','The tracer moves with the fluid and escapes at the leak.~Detected radiation outside the intended pipe route indicates the escaped labelled material.')
add('6.1.2-02-M05','A thickness monitor measures radiation transmitted through a sheet.','For a suitable radiation and range, a thicker sheet gives a smaller transmitted count under fixed source and geometry.','Beta radiation is a common school example for thin-sheet monitoring because partial absorption can respond usefully to thickness. An alpha source may be stopped too readily and a very penetrating gamma beam may change too little for that particular task. Match the radiation to the actual material rather than memorising a fixed choice for every sheet.','Explain how a transmission count can reveal increased sheet thickness.','More material attenuates more of the suitable incident radiation.~The detector count falls when source and geometry remain unchanged.')
add('6.1.2-02-M06','Suitable nuclear fuel can release energy by controlled fission.','Heating can drive a turbine and generator to give electrical output.','The isotope’s nuclear properties permit the intended chain reaction; not every radioactive source is a practical power-reactor fuel. Connect released energy to the engineering stages and include cooling and radioactive-waste management as consequences. This application links to the earlier energy-source explanation rather than replacing the nuclear equations taught in this atomic topic.','Explain the energy path from suitable fission fuel to electrical output.','Controlled fission releases energy that heats a working fluid.~The fluid drives a turbine coupled to a generator.')
add('6.1.2-02-M07','Radiation can sterilise suitable materials by damaging microorganisms.','Penetrating radiation can reach some packaged materials without opening the package.','The absorbed radiation produces the biological effect. The suitability of packaging, exposure and material must be controlled by the intended process. Sterilisation does not mean the package itself becomes an active gamma source merely because radiation passed through it in this model.','Explain a benefit of penetrating radiation for sterilising suitable packaged equipment.','It can reach microorganisms through the packaging.~Absorption damages them without requiring the package to be opened.')
A.misconception('MIS-22','Nucleon number includes both protons and neutrons.','Subtract proton number to obtain neutron number.','A nucleus with A = 39 and Z = 19 has 19 protons and 20 neutrons, not 39 neutrons. Treating A as neutron count adds the protons twice when reconstructing the nucleus. Reading the definitions before arithmetic keeps the particle roles clear.','A nucleus has A = 39 and Z = 19. Explain the error in calling 39 its neutron number.','The error confuses total nucleons with neutrons alone.~The correct neutron number is A − Z = 20.')
A.misconception('MIS-23','Isotopes share proton number and differ in neutron number.','Same neutron count with different proton counts does not define isotopes of one element.','Two atoms with eight protons and eight or ten neutrons are oxygen isotopes. An atom with nine protons is a different element even if its neutron count matches one of them. The proton count fixes element identity, so exchanging the roles changes the classification.','Two atoms have equal neutron numbers but different proton numbers. Explain why that does not make them isotopes of one element.','Different proton numbers identify different elements.~The correct isotope condition is equal proton number with different neutron and nucleon numbers.')
C=A.choice
C('6.1.1-01','Which particles are nucleons?',('Protons and neutrons','They constitute the nucleus.'),('Electrons and photons','These are not nucleons.'),('Only electrons','Electrons occupy the surrounding atom.'),('Only protons in every nuclide','Most nuclei also contain neutrons.'))
C('6.1.1-02','What fixes an element’s identity?',('Proton number','This is atomic number Z.'),('Neutron number alone','Isotopes can differ in it.'),('The sample’s temperature','Heating need not change the nucleus.'),('The number of atoms in the sample','Sample size does not set element identity.'))
C('6.1.2-01','Which pair can be isotopes?',('Same Z, different A','They share element identity but differ in neutrons.'),('Different Z, same A','They are different elements.'),('Same neutrons, different protons','Different protons mean different elements.'),('Different electric charge only','That can describe ions of the same nuclide.'))
C('6.1.1-05-M01','What is an alpha particle?',('Two protons and two neutrons','It is a helium-4 nucleus.'),('A single electron','That is beta-minus radiation.'),('An electromagnetic photon','That is gamma radiation.'),('A neutron alone','An alpha has four nucleons.'))
C('6.1.2-02-M04','What makes a tracer useful for locating a leak?',('It follows the fluid and can be detected','Its movement and signal reveal location.'),('It must block the pipe completely','That changes the flow rather than traces it.'),('Its charge must become zero instantly','That is not the detection principle.'),('The detector must create the leak','The detector observes radiation.'))
C('6.1.2-02-M05','A sheet becomes thicker under a fixed suitable transmission monitor. What is expected?',('Lower transmitted count','More radiation is attenuated.'),('Higher count because the source grows','The source is unchanged.'),('No possible change in transmission','Thickness affects attenuation.'),('The detector becomes the isotope','Detector and source are different.'))
C('6.1.2-02-M01','Carbon-14 dating is most directly suited to which model sample?',('Once-living carbon-containing material','Its post-exchange decay supplies the clock.'),('A newly cast pure metal with no relevant carbon history','The required clock is not established.'),('A shadow on a wall','It is not a material carbon sample.'),('An arbitrary vacuum gap','It contains no sample.'))
C('6.1.1-03','A = 23 and Z = 11. How many neutrons?',('12','Subtract Z from A.'),('23','This is total nucleons.'),('11','This is proton count.'),('34','This adds instead of subtracting.'))
C('6.1.1-05-M01','Alpha decay starts with A = 210 and Z = 84. What are daughter numbers?',('A = 206, Z = 82','Subtract four and two.'),('A = 210, Z = 85','That is the beta-minus change.'),('A = 208, Z = 80','The subtractions are incorrect.'),('A = 214, Z = 86','That adds the emitted particle instead of removing it.'))
C('6.1.1-05-M02','Beta-minus decay starts with A = 14 and Z = 6. What daughter results?',('A = 14, Z = 7','A neutron becomes a proton.'),('A = 10, Z = 4','That is the alpha change.'),('A = 14, Z = 5','That is the wrong beta-minus charge direction.'),('A = 13, Z = 6','Nucleon count is unchanged.'))
C('6.1.2-01','Atoms have 6 protons each and 6 or 8 neutrons. What follows?',('They are isotopes','They have common Z and different A.'),('They are different elements','Their Z is equal.'),('Their A is equal','Their neutron numbers differ.'),('Both must be radioactive by definition','Isotope does not imply radioactivity.'))
C('6.1.2-02-M01','A simple dating model leaves one quarter of initial activity. How many half-lives elapsed?',('Two','One half then one quarter.'),('One','That leaves one half.'),('Four','That leaves one sixteenth.'),('Zero','The activity has decreased.'))
P=A.part
